// Practice quiz: Composition over inheritance
// Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.
// GIFT forbids the angle-bracket characters, and code is described in words where GIFT syntax would clash.

$CATEGORY: java-foundations/inheritance-and-design/composition-over-inheritance

::o1 delegate::A Logger class wants to write to a file without becoming a kind of file writer. Which design follows composition? {
=Logger holds a writer field and forwards its calls to it#Correct. Delegation reuses the writer's behaviour without inheriting its whole public surface.
~Logger extends the writer class#Not quite. That makes Logger a writer, exposing methods it never meant to offer.
~Logger copies the writer's source code#Not quite. Copying duplicates code that delegation would reuse.
~The writer extends Logger#Not quite. That reverses the dependency and still uses inheritance.
}

::o1 swap the part::Car holds an Engine field typed by an interface. What does that make easy? {
~Calling Engine's private methods from Car#Not quite. Composition does not grant access to private members.
~Letting Car be passed wherever an Engine is expected#Not quite. Car is not an Engine; it has one.
=Giving a Car a different Engine implementation without changing Car#Correct. A held part can be swapped, often through the constructor.
~Avoiding any constructor#Not quite. Something still has to supply the Engine.
}

::o2 has-a::Which statement describes has-a rather than is-a? {
~A Laptop is a Computer#Not quite. is a describes a subtype.
=A Library has many Books#Correct. A Library is not a kind of Book, so it holds them.
~A Rectangle is a Shape#Not quite. That is an is-a relationship.
~An ArrayList is a List#Not quite. ArrayList implements List, which is is-a.
}

::o2 test::Which quick check suggests composition instead of inheritance? {
~X and Y share at least one method name#Not quite. Shared names alone say nothing about the relationship.
~Y is declared public#Not quite. Access modifiers do not decide whether X should inherit Y or hold one.
~X is written after Y#Not quite. Writing order does not decide the design.
=The phrase X is a kind of Y sounds wrong, but X needs Y's help#Correct. If X only uses Y, hold Y as a field.
}

::o3 deep chains::Why does a change in the top class of a five-level chain tend to be risky? {
~Java limits a chain to four levels#Not quite. There is no such limit.
~Top classes cannot be edited#Not quite. They can, but the change spreads to every class below.
=Every subclass below inherits the change, including ones that relied on the old behaviour#Correct. Each subclass depends on details of all the classes above it.
~Subclasses copy the old version when compiled#Not quite. Subclasses use the superclass as it is at run time.
}

::o3 fragile base::A superclass method calls another of its own methods that a subclass overrides. What can go wrong when the superclass changes? {
~Nothing; overriding isolates subclasses#Not quite. Overrides are exactly where internal changes leak through.
=The subclass's override may start or stop running in ways its author did not expect#Correct. Subclasses depend on the superclass's internal call pattern, not just its public contract.
~The override is ignored from then on#Not quite. It still runs whenever the superclass calls that method.
~The program stops compiling#Not quite. It usually compiles; the break shows up in behaviour.
}
